cff-version: "1.2.0"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it using the article information in CITATION.cff."

title: "Structural Assurability: A Claim-Relative Framework for Evidence-Based AI Assurance"

authors:
  - family-names: Case
    given-names: Denise M.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6165-7389"
    affiliation: "Northwest Missouri State University, School of Computer Science and Information Systems, Maryville, MO, USA"

repository-code: "https://github.com/structural-explainability/paper-320-structural-assurability"
license: "CC-BY-4.0"

abstract: >-
  This repository contains the source materials for Structural Assurability:
  A Claim-Relative Framework for Evidence-Based AI Assurance. The paper
  formalizes structural assurability as the claim-relative capacity of a
  system and its evidence-producing environment to make assurance-relevant
  evidence obtainable under specified access, cooperation, and resource
  constraints. Systems are compared through the claim-relevant distinctions
  their obtainable evidence enables, permitting equivalence, strict
  dominance, and incomparability without a scalar score. Seven candidate
  structural dimensions are proposed. The companion Lean 4 formalization
  at v0.4.1 provides profile-grounded separating constructions for
  observability, evidence coverage, traceability, and reconstructability.
  Capability non-equivalence is a separate obligation.
  Controllability, independence, and integrity remain open candidates.
  Representative AI architectures show that richer instrumentation
  can increase assurability for some claims, add nothing for others, and
  trade internal visibility against independent evidence.
  The framework characterizes capacity for examination,
  not a favorable assurance outcome.

keywords:
  - structural explainability
  - structural assurability
  - AI assurance
  - assurance evidence
  - assurance cases
  - observability
  - controllability
  - traceability
  - reconstructability
  - evidence integrity
  - auditability
  - runtime assurance
  - comparative assurability

